*** Dropbox (Google drive)**


It is used to store and share files across devices


1.a Functional requirements 

upload a file to remote storage 
download a file 
sync files automatically across devices (my local file should be updated to remote file)

1.b Non-functional requirements

A >> C (its okay if someone uploads a file in US and someone views it in India its still old form)
Low latency upload/download
Support large files as much as 50 GB (for this we need to allow resumable downloads)


2. Core Entities

File (raw byte)
File Metadata
Users

3. API (satisfy all functional requirements and uses core entity)

> upload

POST /file/upload --> 200
Header : User Info(JWT/Session token)
body : file and filedata

> download 

Get /file/:fileId --> File & FileMetadata

 > Sync 

 Get /changes?since={timestamp} --> Files[] 

 The returned list of files would be passed to download to sync 


 4. HLD 

Client --> LB & Gateway (Auth/ SSL termination/ Rate limit/Routing) --post--> File Service ---> Blob Storage(ex S3) {for actual file raw byte}
                                                                                            ---> DB (to store filemetadata) (id, type, size, ..., s3 link)

For actual file raw byte we can use blob storage like s3 it would give us a link and that we can store in our db in file metadata

s3 internally divides the files into various chunks and stores it in various storage nodes , it replicates to ensure high availability and durability , the 
replication is done usually via erasure coding (which adds parity bits)


For get flow --GET(fileId) --> Look up DB to get S3 link return S3 link to client where file can be downloaded (we can have CDN etc for lower latency)

For sync flow , our client actually has logic in this type of design (they will have a local folder + app + localdb(to store file metadata) , app is responsible for syncing)

Two types of sync are needed

remote changed --> we will use our file service to send out push notifications(via long polling/websocket) of metadata to local app which checks in local db if metadata has changed if it is i will download the change 

local changed --> we use native api provided by OS (ex FileSystemWatcher by windows) once change occur we upload them via normal upload process


5. Deep dives

Lets go over non-functional requirments 

> Support large files with resumable downloads

Current design wont work for uploading the large files simply because request bodies dont support this much we have much shorter limits

First what we would do is upload directly to s3 using presigned urls , but we still have one issue which is of time uploading a 50 gb file at normal speed can take upto hour , so what happens if user looses internet halfway to combat this we divide the file into "chunks" at client level , each chunk is of say 5 mb we will store chunks and their status in our db and only upload the missing if we break in middle 

To unique identify chunks , we run a hash over actual bytes of file it gives us their fingerprint

So to summarise when user wants to upload a file we chunk their file send it to file service it gets stored in file metadata we request a presigned url from 
s3 provide it back to client where upload starts happening

*** To summarise (handling large file used at other places like yt etc also) ***

The client will chunk the file into 5-10Mb pieces and calculate a fingerprint for each chunk. It will also calculate a fingerprint for the entire file, this becomes the fileId.
The client will send a GET request to fetch the FileMetadata for the file with the given fileId (fingerprint) in order to see if it already exists -- in which case, we can resume the upload.
If the file does not exist, the client will POST a request to /files/presigned-url to get a presigned URL for the file. The backend will save the file metadata in the FileMetadata table with a status of "uploading" and the chunks array will be a list of the chunk fingerprints with a status of "not-uploaded".
The client will then upload each chunk to S3 using the presigned URL. After each chunk is uploaded, S3 will send a message to our backend using S3 event notifications. Our backend will then update the chunks field in the FileMetadata table to mark the chunk as "uploaded".
Once all chunks in our chunks array are marked as "uploaded", the backend will update the FileMetadata table to mark the file as "uploaded"